# LINE ENDINGS: this repo is LF, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# `eol=lf` takes precedence over core.autocrlf, so it holds whatever a
# contributor has configured globally. That matters here because the tooling
# writes LF unconditionally: bash heredocs, the patch scripts in the
# scratchpad, and the OBJ exporter all join lines with a bare LF. With a CRLF
# working tree, appending to a file left it HALF AND HALF, and nothing warned.
#
# Three ways that bit, all of them observed in this repo:
#   1. Patch scripts matched NOTHING. They sniff a file's convention; a mixed
#      file answers "CRLF" while the lines being targeted are LF.
#   2. Files drifted. A script that picked CRLF rewrote the LF lines too,
#      flipping a file a few lines at a time.
#   3. An unreviewable commit. page.tsx once staged as 21,789 insertions /
#      21,205 deletions for a 172-line change -- 21,315 carriage returns its
#      parent did not have. A diff like that destroys review and git blame.
#
# A guard test (lineEndings.test.ts) fails on any tracked file that goes
# mixed, so this is enforced rather than merely documented.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are LF or they do not run.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF. None are
# tracked today; this is here so the first one added is correct.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The OBJ golden fixture is a byte-for-byte record of what the exporter
# writes, and the exporter always joins lines with "\n". With core.autocrlf
# on (the default on Windows), a checkout would rewrite it to CRLF and the
# golden comparison would fail for a reason that has nothing to do with the
# exporter. Never let git touch its line endings.
packages/panel-catalog/src/objGolden.fixture.obj -text
